window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"][63] = "<h1>Glossary</h1>\n<p>236 terms. Each term is defined once, here and in its owning chapter; chapters link to it and may extend the idea but never redefine it.</p>\n<p><strong>Terms by owning chapter</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>C01</strong> The APM Role and How This Book Works: <a href=\"#gl-decision-rights\">decision rights</a>, <a href=\"#gl-product-triad\">product triad</a>, <a href=\"#gl-rotational-program\">rotational program</a></li>\n<li><strong>C02</strong> Product Thinking and Decision Foundations: <a href=\"#gl-cost-of-delay\">cost of delay</a>, <a href=\"#gl-first-principles-reasoning\">first-principles reasoning</a>, <a href=\"#gl-inversion\">inversion</a>, <a href=\"#gl-local-optimum\">local optimum</a>, <a href=\"#gl-pre-mortem\">pre-mortem</a>, <a href=\"#gl-product-thinking\">product thinking</a>, <a href=\"#gl-reversible-decision\">reversible decision</a>, <a href=\"#gl-second-order-effect\">second-order effect</a>, <a href=\"#gl-sunk-cost\">sunk cost</a>, <a href=\"#gl-tradeoff\">tradeoff</a></li>\n<li><strong>C03</strong> Structured Problem Solving: MECE, Issue Trees, Hypotheses: <a href=\"#gl-five-whys\">five whys</a>, <a href=\"#gl-framework-theater\">framework theater</a>, <a href=\"#gl-hypothesis\">hypothesis</a>, <a href=\"#gl-issue-tree\">issue tree</a>, <a href=\"#gl-mece\">MECE</a>, <a href=\"#gl-pareto-principle\">Pareto principle</a>, <a href=\"#gl-root-cause\">root cause</a>, <a href=\"#gl-symptom\">symptom</a></li>\n<li><strong>C04</strong> Estimation Under Pressure: <a href=\"#gl-anchor\">anchor</a>, <a href=\"#gl-estimation\">estimation</a>, <a href=\"#gl-order-of-magnitude\">order of magnitude</a>, <a href=\"#gl-sanity-check\">sanity check</a>, <a href=\"#gl-sensitivity-analysis\">sensitivity analysis</a></li>\n<li><strong>C05</strong> Metrics: Choosing, Defining and Reading Them: <a href=\"#gl-activation\">activation</a>, <a href=\"#gl-churn\">churn</a>, <a href=\"#gl-cohort\">cohort</a>, <a href=\"#gl-conversion-rate\">conversion rate</a>, <a href=\"#gl-counter-metric\">counter metric</a>, <a href=\"#gl-daily-and-monthly-active-users\">daily and monthly active users</a>, <a href=\"#gl-funnel\">funnel</a>, <a href=\"#gl-goodharts-law\">Goodhart's law</a>, <a href=\"#gl-guardrail-metric\">guardrail metric</a>, <a href=\"#gl-input-metric\">input metric</a>, <a href=\"#gl-instrumentation\">instrumentation</a>, <a href=\"#gl-lagging-indicator\">lagging indicator</a>, <a href=\"#gl-leading-indicator\">leading indicator</a>, <a href=\"#gl-metric\">metric</a>, <a href=\"#gl-metric-tree\">metric tree</a>, <a href=\"#gl-north-star-metric\">north star metric</a>, <a href=\"#gl-proxy-metric\">proxy metric</a>, <a href=\"#gl-retention\">retention</a></li>\n<li><strong>C06</strong> Users, Problems and Discovery: <a href=\"#gl-jobs-to-be-done\">jobs to be done</a>, <a href=\"#gl-persona\">persona</a>, <a href=\"#gl-problem-statement\">problem statement</a>, <a href=\"#gl-say-do-gap\">say-do gap</a>, <a href=\"#gl-user-segment\">user segment</a></li>\n<li><strong>C07</strong> Product Design: Designing for a User: <a href=\"#gl-pain-point\">pain point</a>, <a href=\"#gl-product-sense\">product sense</a></li>\n<li><strong>C08</strong> Improving an Existing Product: <a href=\"#gl-cannibalization\">cannibalization</a>, <a href=\"#gl-headroom\">headroom</a>, <a href=\"#gl-legacy-user\">legacy user</a></li>\n<li><strong>C09</strong> New Markets and Underserved Users: <a href=\"#gl-localization\">localization</a>, <a href=\"#gl-trust-signal\">trust signal</a>, <a href=\"#gl-underserved-user\">underserved user</a></li>\n<li><strong>C10</strong> UX and Product Quality: <a href=\"#gl-accessibility\">accessibility</a>, <a href=\"#gl-cognitive-load\">cognitive load</a>, <a href=\"#gl-dark-pattern\">dark pattern</a>, <a href=\"#gl-information-architecture\">information architecture</a>, <a href=\"#gl-nudge\">nudge</a>, <a href=\"#gl-usability-heuristic\">usability heuristic</a></li>\n<li><strong>C11</strong> Product Critique and Teardown Method: <a href=\"#gl-first-run-experience\">first-run experience</a>, <a href=\"#gl-teardown\">teardown</a></li>\n<li><strong>C12</strong> Statistics for PMs: <a href=\"#gl-base-rate\">base rate</a>, <a href=\"#gl-confidence-interval\">confidence interval</a>, <a href=\"#gl-effect-size\">effect size</a>, <a href=\"#gl-multiple-comparisons\">multiple comparisons</a>, <a href=\"#gl-p-value\">p-value</a>, <a href=\"#gl-percentile\">percentile</a>, <a href=\"#gl-practical-significance\">practical significance</a>, <a href=\"#gl-regression-to-the-mean\">regression to the mean</a>, <a href=\"#gl-simpsons-paradox\">Simpson's paradox</a>, <a href=\"#gl-standard-error\">standard error</a>, <a href=\"#gl-statistical-power\">statistical power</a>, <a href=\"#gl-survivorship-bias\">survivorship bias</a></li>\n<li><strong>C13</strong> Diagnosing a Metric Change: <a href=\"#gl-baseline\">baseline</a>, <a href=\"#gl-grain\">grain</a>, <a href=\"#gl-metric-diagnosis\">metric diagnosis</a>, <a href=\"#gl-mix-shift\">mix shift</a>, <a href=\"#gl-pull-forward\">pull-forward</a>, <a href=\"#gl-seasonality\">seasonality</a>, <a href=\"#gl-sql\">SQL</a></li>\n<li><strong>C14</strong> Experiment Design and Causal Reasoning: <a href=\"#gl-a-b-test\">A/B test</a>, <a href=\"#gl-causal-effect\">causal effect</a>, <a href=\"#gl-confounder\">confounder</a>, <a href=\"#gl-counterfactual\">counterfactual</a>, <a href=\"#gl-difference-in-differences\">difference-in-differences</a>, <a href=\"#gl-experiment\">experiment</a>, <a href=\"#gl-holdout\">holdout</a>, <a href=\"#gl-interference\">interference</a>, <a href=\"#gl-minimum-detectable-effect\">minimum detectable effect</a>, <a href=\"#gl-novelty-effect\">novelty effect</a>, <a href=\"#gl-peeking\">peeking</a>, <a href=\"#gl-randomization\">randomization</a>, <a href=\"#gl-sample-ratio-mismatch\">sample ratio mismatch</a></li>\n<li><strong>C15</strong> Prioritization and Tradeoffs: <a href=\"#gl-assumption\">assumption</a>, <a href=\"#gl-mvp\">minimum viable product</a>, <a href=\"#gl-opportunity-cost\">opportunity cost</a>, <a href=\"#gl-prioritization\">prioritization</a>, <a href=\"#gl-scope-creep\">scope creep</a>, <a href=\"#gl-time-box\">time-box</a></li>\n<li><strong>C16</strong> Specs, Engineering and Design Partnership: <a href=\"#gl-acceptance-criteria\">acceptance criteria</a>, <a href=\"#gl-backlog\">backlog</a>, <a href=\"#gl-definition-of-done\">definition of done</a>, <a href=\"#gl-non-goal\">non-goal</a>, <a href=\"#gl-prd\">product requirements document</a>, <a href=\"#gl-sprint\">sprint</a>, <a href=\"#gl-technical-debt\">technical debt</a>, <a href=\"#gl-user-story\">user story</a></li>\n<li><strong>C17</strong> Launch, Rollout and Execution Decisions: <a href=\"#gl-blast-radius\">blast radius</a>, <a href=\"#gl-canary-release\">canary release</a>, <a href=\"#gl-feature-flag\">feature flag</a>, <a href=\"#gl-postmortem\">postmortem</a>, <a href=\"#gl-rollback\">rollback</a>, <a href=\"#gl-rollback-trigger\">rollback trigger</a>, <a href=\"#gl-staged-rollout\">staged rollout</a></li>\n<li><strong>C18</strong> Strategy at APM Depth: <a href=\"#gl-contribution-margin\">contribution margin</a>, <a href=\"#gl-moat\">moat</a>, <a href=\"#gl-network-effect\">network effect</a>, <a href=\"#gl-okr\">OKR</a>, <a href=\"#gl-positioning\">positioning</a>, <a href=\"#gl-roadmap\">roadmap</a>, <a href=\"#gl-strategy\">strategy</a>, <a href=\"#gl-switching-cost\">switching cost</a>, <a href=\"#gl-total-addressable-market\">total addressable market</a>, <a href=\"#gl-unit-economics\">unit economics</a></li>\n<li><strong>C19</strong> Platforms and Ecosystems: <a href=\"#gl-cold-start-problem\">cold-start problem</a>, <a href=\"#gl-developer-experience\">developer experience</a>, <a href=\"#gl-disintermediation\">disintermediation</a>, <a href=\"#gl-multi-homing\">multi-homing</a>, <a href=\"#gl-platform\">platform</a>, <a href=\"#gl-take-rate\">take rate</a>, <a href=\"#gl-two-sided-market\">two-sided market</a></li>\n<li><strong>C20</strong> Go-to-Market, Pricing and Growth Basics: <a href=\"#gl-customer-acquisition-cost\">customer acquisition cost</a>, <a href=\"#gl-go-to-market\">go-to-market</a>, <a href=\"#gl-growth-loop\">growth loop</a>, <a href=\"#gl-lifetime-value\">lifetime value</a>, <a href=\"#gl-packaging\">packaging</a>, <a href=\"#gl-payback-period\">payback period</a>, <a href=\"#gl-product-led-growth\">product-led growth</a>, <a href=\"#gl-value-metric\">value metric</a>, <a href=\"#gl-willingness-to-pay\">willingness to pay</a></li>\n<li><strong>C21</strong> How Products Work: Technical Fluency: <a href=\"#gl-api\">API</a>, <a href=\"#gl-availability\">availability</a>, <a href=\"#gl-cache\">cache</a>, <a href=\"#gl-data-pipeline\">data pipeline</a>, <a href=\"#gl-database-index\">database index</a>, <a href=\"#gl-idempotency\">idempotency</a>, <a href=\"#gl-latency\">latency</a>, <a href=\"#gl-message-queue\">message queue</a>, <a href=\"#gl-on-device-processing\">on-device processing</a>, <a href=\"#gl-schema\">schema</a>, <a href=\"#gl-service-level-objective\">service level objective</a>, <a href=\"#gl-throughput\">throughput</a></li>\n<li><strong>C22</strong> PM-Level System Design: <a href=\"#gl-abuse-case\">abuse case</a>, <a href=\"#gl-eventual-consistency\">eventual consistency</a>, <a href=\"#gl-fan-out\">fan-out</a>, <a href=\"#gl-graceful-degradation\">graceful degradation</a>, <a href=\"#gl-rate-limiting\">rate limiting</a>, <a href=\"#gl-webhook\">webhook</a></li>\n<li><strong>C23</strong> Machine Learning, Ranking and Recommendation for PMs: <a href=\"#gl-candidate-generation\">candidate generation</a>, <a href=\"#gl-embedding\">embedding</a>, <a href=\"#gl-exploration\">exploration</a>, <a href=\"#gl-feedback-loop\">feedback loop</a>, <a href=\"#gl-label\">label</a>, <a href=\"#gl-model\">model</a>, <a href=\"#gl-offline-evaluation\">offline evaluation</a>, <a href=\"#gl-overfitting\">overfitting</a>, <a href=\"#gl-popularity-bias\">popularity bias</a>, <a href=\"#gl-position-bias\">position bias</a>, <a href=\"#gl-precision-recall\">precision and recall</a>, <a href=\"#gl-ranking\">ranking</a>, <a href=\"#gl-recommender-system\">recommender system</a>, <a href=\"#gl-threshold\">threshold</a>, <a href=\"#gl-training-data\">training data</a></li>\n<li><strong>C24</strong> AI Product Management: Foundations: <a href=\"#gl-agent\">agent</a>, <a href=\"#gl-context-window\">context window</a>, <a href=\"#gl-fine-tuning\">fine-tuning</a>, <a href=\"#gl-grounding\">grounding</a>, <a href=\"#gl-hallucination\">hallucination</a>, <a href=\"#gl-human-in-the-loop\">human in the loop</a>, <a href=\"#gl-llm\">large language model</a>, <a href=\"#gl-prompt\">prompt</a>, <a href=\"#gl-prompt-injection\">prompt injection</a>, <a href=\"#gl-rag\">retrieval-augmented generation</a>, <a href=\"#gl-token\">token</a></li>\n<li><strong>C25</strong> AI Product Management: Building, Evaluating and Governing: <a href=\"#gl-drift\">drift</a>, <a href=\"#gl-eval\">eval</a>, <a href=\"#gl-guardrail\">guardrail</a>, <a href=\"#gl-inter-rater-agreement\">inter-rater agreement</a>, <a href=\"#gl-model-card\">model card</a>, <a href=\"#gl-model-graded-eval\">model-graded eval</a>, <a href=\"#gl-red-teaming\">red-teaming</a>, <a href=\"#gl-regression-test\">regression test</a>, <a href=\"#gl-risk-tier\">risk tier</a>, <a href=\"#gl-shadow-mode\">shadow mode</a></li>\n<li><strong>C26</strong> 0→1 and Idea Evaluation: <a href=\"#gl-concierge-test\">concierge test</a>, <a href=\"#gl-kill-criteria\">kill criteria</a>, <a href=\"#gl-pivot\">pivot</a>, <a href=\"#gl-product-market-fit\">product-market fit</a>, <a href=\"#gl-riskiest-assumption\">riskiest assumption</a>, <a href=\"#gl-smoke-test\">smoke test</a></li>\n<li><strong>C27</strong> Domain Primers: <a href=\"#gl-ad-auction\">ad auction</a>, <a href=\"#gl-chargeback\">chargeback</a>, <a href=\"#gl-internal-platform\">internal platform</a>, <a href=\"#gl-know-your-customer\">know your customer</a>, <a href=\"#gl-liquidity\">liquidity</a>, <a href=\"#gl-net-revenue-retention\">net revenue retention</a>, <a href=\"#gl-prevalence\">prevalence</a>, <a href=\"#gl-sales-cycle\">sales cycle</a>, <a href=\"#gl-trust-and-safety\">trust and safety</a></li>\n<li><strong>C29</strong> Anti-Patterns: <a href=\"#gl-anti-pattern\">anti-pattern</a>, <a href=\"#gl-feature-factory\">feature factory</a>, <a href=\"#gl-hippo\">HiPPO</a>, <a href=\"#gl-vanity-metric\">vanity metric</a></li>\n<li><strong>C31</strong> Behavioral Interviews: Stories That Hold Up: <a href=\"#gl-signal\">signal</a>, <a href=\"#gl-story-bank\">story bank</a></li>\n<li><strong>C32</strong> Communication and Influence at APM Level: <a href=\"#gl-bluf\">bottom line up front</a>, <a href=\"#gl-decision-document\">decision document</a>, <a href=\"#gl-escalation\">escalation</a>, <a href=\"#gl-influence-without-authority\">influence without authority</a>, <a href=\"#gl-pyramid-principle\">pyramid principle</a>, <a href=\"#gl-stakeholder\">stakeholder</a></li>\n<li><strong>C33</strong> Interview Loops and Company Lenses: <a href=\"#gl-bar-raiser\">bar raiser</a>, <a href=\"#gl-company-brief\">company brief</a>, <a href=\"#gl-hiring-committee\">hiring committee</a>, <a href=\"#gl-interview-loop\">interview loop</a></li>\n<li><strong>C34</strong> Mock Interviews, Scoring and Calibration: <a href=\"#gl-calibration\">calibration</a>, <a href=\"#gl-deliberate-practice\">deliberate practice</a>, <a href=\"#gl-error-log\">error log</a>, <a href=\"#gl-mock-interview\">mock interview</a>, <a href=\"#gl-scorecard\">scorecard</a></li>\n<li><strong>C37</strong> The 12-Month Plan, Readiness Checklist and Your First 90 Days: <a href=\"#gl-readiness-gate\">readiness gate</a>, <a href=\"#gl-reading-map\">reading map</a></li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-entries\"></span>\n<h2>Entries</h2>\n<span id=\"gl-a-b-test\"></span>\n<h3>A/B test</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> An experiment in which users are randomly assigned to the current version (A) or a changed version (B) and the same metric is compared between the two groups.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> Half of new visitors see the current checkout and half see the new one; the share who buy is compared.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> Comparing sign-ups before and after a launch.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C14</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-abuse-case\"></span>\n<h3>abuse case</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> A way a system could be used to harm someone, analyzed like a requirement: who might misuse it, how, the harm, and the control that reduces it.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> A small tracker slipped into a bag to follow a person, with an alert to the person being followed as the control.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> An ordinary bug that affects only the person who triggered it.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C22</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-acceptance-criteria\"></span>\n<h3>acceptance criteria</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> The specific, testable conditions a story must meet to count as done, often written as given a situation, when an action, then an observable result.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> Given a list shared as view only, when a visitor opens the link, then they see the items and cannot change them.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> 'It should work well.'</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C16</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-accessibility\"></span>\n<h3>accessibility</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> The degree to which a product can be used by people with the widest range of abilities, including through assistive technology.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> Labels that a screen reader can speak, text with enough contrast and controls operable by keyboard or voice.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> An accessibility statement on a website with no accessible controls.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C10</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-activation\"></span>\n<h3>activation</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> The point at which a new user first experiences the product's core value, defined by an observable action.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> Sending a first message to a teammate.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> Creating an account.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C05</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-ad-auction\"></span>\n<h3>ad auction</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> The mechanism by which an advertising platform decides, each time a slot is available, which ad to show and at what price, typically ranking advertisers by their bid combined with the predicted chance the user responds and charging the winner the minimum needed to keep its position.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> A $2.00 bid with a 1.0% predicted click rate beats a $3.00 bid with 0.5% and pays $1.80 a click.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> Showing the ad with the highest bid and charging that bid.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C27</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-agent\"></span>\n<h3>agent</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> A system that uses a model to plan and take actions through tools in pursuit of a goal.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> An assistant that reads a calendar, proposes times, and sends invitations.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> A model that answers one question and stops.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C24</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-anchor\"></span>\n<h3>anchor</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> A well-known quantity used as the starting point for an estimate.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> The number of seconds in a day, or the population of a country.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> A number guessed with no basis and presented as fact.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C04</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-anti-pattern\"></span>\n<h3>anti-pattern</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> A common response to a recurring problem that looks sensible, is easy to adopt and reliably produces a worse result than the alternatives; the term names the response, not the person.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> Judging a team by the number of features shipped instead of the outcomes they change.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> A one-off mistake with no recurring cause.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C29</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-api\"></span>\n<h3>API</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> A defined way for one piece of software to request data or actions from another.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> A weather service returning a forecast when sent a city name.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> A website's visual design.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C21</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-assumption\"></span>\n<h3>assumption</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> A belief a plan depends on that has not yet been verified.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> &quot;Users will pay for faster delivery.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> A verified measurement.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C15</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-availability\"></span>\n<h3>availability</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> The share of time a service works as intended, usually stated in nines; availabilities of services in a chain multiply.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> 99.9% availability allows about 43.8 minutes of downtime in a month.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> The speed of a single response.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C21</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-backlog\"></span>\n<h3>backlog</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> The single list of work a team could do next, ranked by the product manager against the goal.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> A list of 40 items in priority order whose top ten are clear, estimated and testable.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> A pile of unranked requests from many people.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C16</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-bar-raiser\"></span>\n<h3>bar raiser</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> An interviewer from outside the hiring team, trained to hold a consistent hiring standard across teams, who in some processes can block a hire the team wants (as candidates report for one company).</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> An outside interviewer in the loop who probes stories for detail and writes a separate assessment.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> The hiring manager of the team that is recruiting.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C33</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-base-rate\"></span>\n<h3>base rate</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> How common something is in the population before any new evidence is considered.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> If 1 in 1,000 accounts is fraudulent, a flag must be judged against that rate.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> The accuracy of the detection model.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C12</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-baseline\"></span>\n<h3>baseline</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> The value a metric would be expected to take without the event under investigation, estimated from recent history and the same period in earlier cycles.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> This Tuesday's orders compared with the average of recent Tuesdays and with the same holiday week last year.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> Yesterday's value, which carries the day-of-week effect and one day's noise.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C13</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-blast-radius\"></span>\n<h3>blast radius</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> How many users or systems a failure would affect.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> A bug behind a flag at 1% exposure affects 10,000 of a million users; the same bug at 100% affects all of them.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> The probability that a failure occurs.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C17</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-bluf\"></span>\n<h3>bottom line up front</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> The practice of stating the conclusion or request first and the supporting detail after it.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> I recommend we delay the launch one week because the checkout bug affects 3% of orders; I need your approval by Thursday.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> Two minutes of background before the point.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C32</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-cache\"></span>\n<h3>cache</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> A stored copy of data kept close to where it is needed so it can be served faster than recomputing or refetching it, at the cost of possible staleness.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> Product details served from memory for a few minutes instead of querying the database each time.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> The system of record that holds the authoritative data.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C21</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-calibration\"></span>\n<h3>calibration</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> The alignment of your self-assessment with outside judgment, measured as bias (mean of your score minus the outside score) and error (mean absolute difference).</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> A candidate whose self-scores run 0.75 points above a partner's over eight mocks and who corrects for it.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> Feeling confident that you are ready.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C34</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-canary-release\"></span>\n<h3>canary release</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> A first release of a change to a small subset of users or servers that acts as an early warning before wider exposure.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> Showing the rewritten account page to 1% of users for a business day while watching errors.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> A release to all users with monitoring added afterward.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C17</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-candidate-generation\"></span>\n<h3>candidate generation</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> The cheap first stage of a recommender or search system that narrows a huge catalog to a manageable set of plausible items for the heavier ranking stage.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> Narrowing 100 million videos to about 1,000 candidates using similarity, follows and trending.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> The final ordering of the items shown.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C23</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-cannibalization\"></span>\n<h3>cannibalization</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> When a new feature or offer takes away use or revenue from an existing one of the same company, so that gross gains overstate net gains.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> A cheaper plan that mostly attracts existing full-price subscribers.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> A new plan whose subscribers are all people who were not customers before.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C08</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-causal-effect\"></span>\n<h3>causal effect</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> The difference in outcome caused by a change, compared with what would have happened without it.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> The extra purchases that occurred because of the new recommendation module.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> Purchases by users who used the module.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C14</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-chargeback\"></span>\n<h3>chargeback</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> A reversal of a card payment forced by the cardholder's bank after a dispute, usually with a fee, and a main cost of fraud.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> A $60 purchase reversed with a $15 fee.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> A refund the merchant chooses to give.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C27</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-churn\"></span>\n<h3>churn</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> The share of users or customers lost in a period, the complement of retention.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> 4% of subscribers cancelling in a month.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> The number of new subscribers added.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C05</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-cognitive-load\"></span>\n<h3>cognitive load</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> The mental effort a task demands of working memory; interfaces that add unneeded effort slow people and cause errors.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> A 22-toggle list with no grouping that makes people scan and remember.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> A short, grouped list with clear labels.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C10</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-cohort\"></span>\n<h3>cohort</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> A group of users who share a starting event or period, tracked together over time.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> Everyone who signed up in March.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> All users active today.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C05</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-cold-start-problem\"></span>\n<h3>cold-start problem</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> The difficulty a platform faces when its value depends on participants who will not join until the platform is already valuable.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> A marketplace where buyers will not come without sellers and sellers will not come without buyers.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> A mature platform with steady demand and supply.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C19</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-company-brief\"></span>\n<h3>company brief</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> A one-page summary of a company's products, users, strategy, metrics, competitors and recent moves, built from public sources, plus your own angle and questions, used to answer 'why us' and to ground product answers.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> A page with the main products, how the company makes money, two competitors, the last three launches, one critique with an improvement and three questions.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> A list of facts copied from the careers page with no view.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C33</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-concierge-test\"></span>\n<h3>concierge test</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> A test in which the service is delivered manually to a few customers, with little or no software, to learn whether it is wanted, how it should work and what it costs.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> Matching meal plans to 12 customers by hand for 12 weeks at $60 a week.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> A fully automated product launched to everyone.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C26</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-confidence-interval\"></span>\n<h3>confidence interval</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> A range of values consistent with the data at a stated confidence level, produced by a procedure that captures the true value at that rate over repeated samples.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> A lift of 1.2% with a 95% interval from 0.4% to 2.0%.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> A guarantee that the true value is inside this one range.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C12</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-confounder\"></span>\n<h3>confounder</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> A factor linked to both a change and an outcome that makes the observed relationship misleading.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> Heavy users adopt a feature and also retain better, so adoption appears to cause retention.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> A random event affecting all users equally.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C14</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-context-window\"></span>\n<h3>context window</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> The maximum amount of text, in tokens, that a model can consider at once, covering instructions, supplied documents, the conversation so far and the answer.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> A document too long to fit in the window must be split or retrieved in parts.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> The model's training data.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C24</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-contribution-margin\"></span>\n<h3>contribution margin</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> Revenue minus the variable costs of a unit (cost of goods, delivery, payment fees, support), before fixed costs.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> An $80 grocery order with $17.60 gross margin and $12 delivery cost contributes $5.60.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> Net profit after allocated overhead.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C18</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-conversion-rate\"></span>\n<h3>conversion rate</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> The share of a defined population that completes a defined action within a stated window.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> First purchases divided by new visitors within seven days.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> A bare statement that conversion is 3% with no population or window.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C05</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-cost-of-delay\"></span>\n<h3>cost of delay</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> The value lost for each unit of time that a decision or release is postponed.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> A fix that recovers 1% of weekly revenue loses that amount every week it waits.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> The time a task takes to complete.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C02</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-counter-metric\"></span>\n<h3>counter metric</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> A metric paired with a target specifically to expose the damage that optimizing the target could cause.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> Support tickets per order, tracked beside order volume.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> A second goal with its own target.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C05</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-counterfactual\"></span>\n<h3>counterfactual</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> What would have happened to the same units if they had not received the change.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> The retention of treated users had they never seen the feature.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> The retention of a different, unrelated group.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C14</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-customer-acquisition-cost\"></span>\n<h3>customer acquisition cost</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> The average cost to acquire one new customer: sales and marketing spend in a period divided by the new customers acquired in it. Abbreviated CAC.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> $45 per new subscriber including salaries, tools and promotions.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> Only the advertising spend divided by clicks.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C20</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-daily-and-monthly-active-users\"></span>\n<h3>daily and monthly active users</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> The counts of distinct users who did a defined qualifying action on one day (DAU) or in a 30-day window (MAU); DAU divided by MAU shows how often monthly users come back.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> 200,000 daily active users and 1,000,000 monthly active users: one in five monthly users shows up on a given day.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> Total registered accounts, which includes people who never return.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C05</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-dark-pattern\"></span>\n<h3>dark pattern</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> A user-interface design that tricks or pressures people into actions they did not intend, to the benefit of the business.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> A subscription that takes one click to join and many steps across several screens to cancel.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> A short retention step that offers a pause and keeps a one-click exit.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C10</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-data-pipeline\"></span>\n<h3>data pipeline</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> A set of automated steps that moves data from where it is produced to where it is analyzed, collecting, cleaning, transforming and loading it, in batches or as a stream.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> A nightly job that turns raw app events into the tables behind a dashboard.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> A one-off spreadsheet export.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C21</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-database-index\"></span>\n<h3>database index</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> A data structure that lets a database find rows by a column's value without scanning the whole table, at the cost of storage and some write time.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> An index on category and city that turns a 2.1-second query into 12 milliseconds.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> A full table scan.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C21</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-decision-document\"></span>\n<h3>decision document</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> A short written proposal that frames a decision for the people who must make it: the decision needed and deadline, context, options, a recommendation with reasons, what would change it, risks and next steps.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> A one-page memo asking for a choice of trial length by Friday with three options and a recommendation.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> A slide deck that presents findings with no ask.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C32</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-decision-rights\"></span>\n<h3>decision rights</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> The allocation of who makes which decisions on a team, and who must be consulted or informed; rarely written down, and unclear rights cause more conflict than disagreement about substance.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> The PM decides what to build and why; engineering decides how; design decides the interaction.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> A reporting line or job title.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C01</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-definition-of-done\"></span>\n<h3>definition of done</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> The shared checklist a piece of work must meet before it counts as complete, such as code reviewed, tests passing, accessibility checked, instrumented and documented.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> A story is done only when its acceptance tests pass, accessibility is checked and events are logged.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> Done meaning 'the developer says it works.'</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C16</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-deliberate-practice\"></span>\n<h3>deliberate practice</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> A form of practice, described in the research on expertise, in which you work on a specific aspect of performance just beyond your current ability, with immediate feedback and repetition.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> Ten metric-definition drills each checked against a success test and retested after three days.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> Logging hours of unscored, unfocused practice.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C34</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-developer-experience\"></span>\n<h3>developer experience</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> The sum of what it takes for a developer to discover, understand, integrate and succeed with a platform: documentation, examples, software kits, error messages, tooling, support and pricing.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> A first working request within an hour, guided by clear errors and a quick start that runs as written.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> A powerful API with outdated examples and errors that say only 'invalid request.'</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C19</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-difference-in-differences\"></span>\n<h3>difference-in-differences</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> An estimate of an effect as the change over time in a treated group minus the change over time in a comparison group, which removes shared trends under the assumption that the groups would have moved in parallel without the change.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> Conversion up 1.5 points in the treated region and 0.6 points in a comparison region gives an estimated effect of 0.9 points.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> The before-and-after change in the treated region alone.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C14</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-disintermediation\"></span>\n<h3>disintermediation</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> Participants bypassing the platform to transact directly after being matched, which reduces the platform's revenue and data.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> A customer and provider rebooking by phone after a first job found on a marketplace.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> Two users who never found each other through the platform.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C19</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-drift\"></span>\n<h3>drift</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> The change over time in inputs, user behavior or the world that makes a deployed model perform worse than when it was evaluated.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> A support assistant's accuracy falling after the company changes its policies.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> A one-time bug introduced by a release.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C25</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-effect-size\"></span>\n<h3>effect size</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> The size of a difference or relationship, stated in units that can be compared with a decision threshold (absolute points, relative percent, or standardized units).</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> A lift of 0.1 percentage points, which is 2% relative, worth 10,000 purchases a month at the traffic in question.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> A p-value, which says whether a difference can be told from zero and not how large it is.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C12</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-embedding\"></span>\n<h3>embedding</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> A list of numbers that represents an item, user or word so that similar things lie close together, letting a system find similar items quickly.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> Finding the 500 videos nearest to a viewer's recent watches.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> A plain category label.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C23</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-error-log\"></span>\n<h3>error log</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> A running list of the specific mistakes made in practice, each with its cause and the fix to apply, reviewed regularly so that recurring errors become the next drills.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> Spent 20 minutes on users and 3 on metrics; fix: state a clock aloud; retest in 72 hours.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> A vague note to manage time better.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C34</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-escalation\"></span>\n<h3>escalation</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> Taking a problem or disagreement to someone with more authority to resolve it; used well it is a way to get a decision, not a way to win.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> Raising a safety disagreement with the decision-maker openly, with the other person informed.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> Going around a colleague without telling them to win an argument.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C32</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-estimation\"></span>\n<h3>estimation</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> Producing a reasoned approximate number from stated assumptions and a decomposition of the quantity.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> Estimating daily rides in a city from population, share who ride, and rides per rider.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> Recalling a published statistic.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C04</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-eval\"></span>\n<h3>eval</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> A repeatable test set and scoring method used to measure the quality of an AI system's output.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> A set of 300 real support questions with reference answers and a grading rubric.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> Trying a few prompts by hand and judging by feel.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C25</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-eventual-consistency\"></span>\n<h3>eventual consistency</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> A property in which copies of data may differ for a short time but will agree if no new changes arrive.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> A like count that is a few seconds behind on one device.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> An account balance that must be the same everywhere at once.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C22</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-experiment\"></span>\n<h3>experiment</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> A controlled test in which units are randomly assigned to variants so the effect of a change can be measured.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> Half of users see the new checkout and half see the old.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> Comparing this month's sales with last month's.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C14</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-exploration\"></span>\n<h3>exploration</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> Deliberately showing items whose value the system is unsure of, to learn, at some cost to immediate engagement, as opposed to exploiting what it already believes is best.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> Reserving 8% of home-screen slots for unfamiliar songs.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> Always showing the highest-scoring items.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C23</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-fan-out\"></span>\n<h3>fan-out</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> One action causing many deliveries or updates, such as one post reaching a million followers; the main cost driver in feeds and notifications (distinct from the join fan-out in a query).</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> A post written into the feed lists of 200 followers.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> A one-to-one message.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C22</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-feature-factory\"></span>\n<h3>feature factory</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> A team that is rewarded for shipping features rather than for the outcomes the features change; signalled by low outcome-metric coverage on the roadmap and few shipped items reviewed afterward.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> Fourteen launches in a half-year with no one able to say which improved completion.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> A team that ships quickly and reviews each launch against a target.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C29</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-feature-flag\"></span>\n<h3>feature flag</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> A switch that turns functionality on or off for chosen users without shipping a new release.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> Enabling a redesigned page for 5% of users.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> A branch in version control.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C17</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-feedback-loop\"></span>\n<h3>feedback loop</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> A cycle in which a system's outputs become its future inputs, so its own choices shape the data it learns from.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> A recommender shows popular songs, which get played, which trains it to show them more.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> A model trained once on data it did not influence.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C23</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-fine-tuning\"></span>\n<h3>fine-tuning</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> Further training of a model on examples of the desired behavior so that it follows a style, format or task more reliably; it does not reliably add new facts.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> Training a small model on 10,000 labeled support tickets to classify them.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> Pasting a policy into the prompt.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C24</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-first-principles-reasoning\"></span>\n<h3>first-principles reasoning</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> Breaking a problem into its basic facts and rebuilding the answer from them instead of copying an existing solution.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> Pricing a delivery service by building up cost per order from courier time, fuel and platform overhead.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> Copying a competitor's price list.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C02</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-first-run-experience\"></span>\n<h3>first-run experience</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> What a person sees and does from first opening a product or device until they have reached their first real value, including setup, permissions and the first task.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> Unboxing earbuds, pairing them, running a fit test and starting a first listening session.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> The experience of a long-time user who already knows the product.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C11</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-five-whys\"></span>\n<h3>five whys</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> A root-cause technique that asks why repeatedly, usually about five times, until it reaches a cause that can be acted on.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> Tracing a missed notification run back to an expired credential and then to a missing ownership process.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> Stopping at the first answer, or concluding that a person made a mistake.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C03</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-framework-theater\"></span>\n<h3>framework theater</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> Naming or drawing a framework without using it to reach a conclusion.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> Reciting a prioritization acronym and then ranking items by gut feel.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> Scoring each option on stated criteria and choosing from the scores.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C03</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-funnel\"></span>\n<h3>funnel</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> The ordered steps a user passes through to reach an outcome, with drop-off measured at each step.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> Visit, sign up, add payment method, first purchase.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> An unordered list of features and their usage.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C05</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-go-to-market\"></span>\n<h3>go-to-market</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> The plan for how a product reaches its target customers and wins them: who the customer is, the problem and value proposition, positioning and message, price and packaging, channels, launch, and the metrics that show whether it is working.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> A one-page plan for an expense-capture feature naming the target owners, the message, the in-product launch and the success measure.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> A list of marketing tactics with no target or success measure.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C20</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-goodharts-law\"></span>\n<h3>Goodhart's law</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> The observation that when a measure becomes a target it stops being a good measure, because effort shifts to the measure rather than the goal.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> Teams raising notification volume to lift a daily-active count.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> A metric rising because the product genuinely improved.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C05</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-graceful-degradation\"></span>\n<h3>graceful degradation</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> A system's ability to keep providing a reduced but useful service when a part fails, instead of failing completely.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> A product page that loads without recommendations when the recommendation service is down.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> A blank page or an endless spinner when one dependency fails.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C22</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-grain\"></span>\n<h3>grain</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> What one row of a table, or of a query result, represents: one row per user, per event, per order or per user per day; most wrong counts come from not knowing the grain of the tables joined or of the result wanted.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> The events table has one row per user per event, so a user with three events has three rows.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> The number of columns in a table, or its total row count.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C13</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-grounding\"></span>\n<h3>grounding</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> Tying a model's output to supplied sources so that its claims can be traced and checked.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> An answer that quotes the current refund policy and links to it.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> An answer written from the model's memory with no source.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C24</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-growth-loop\"></span>\n<h3>growth loop</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> A closed system in which the output of one cycle becomes the input of the next, so that growth compounds, as when users invite others who then invite more.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> A referral loop with 4 invitations per user and 12% conversion.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> A one-way funnel that must be refilled with paid users at the top each time.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C20</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-guardrail\"></span>\n<h3>guardrail</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> A control placed around a model to keep its behavior within limits, such as input and output filters, rules about what it may do, limits on tools and permissions, and escalation to people.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> An output filter that blocks private account data and a cap on refund amounts.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> A metric that must not worsen in an experiment, which is a guardrail metric.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C25</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-guardrail-metric\"></span>\n<h3>guardrail metric</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> A metric that must not get worse while a team pursues its primary goal.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> Crash rate, during a launch meant to make the app faster.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> The metric the launch is trying to improve.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C05</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-hallucination\"></span>\n<h3>hallucination</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> A model output that is fluent but unsupported by its inputs or by fact.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> A model citing a study that does not exist.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> A correct answer phrased awkwardly.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C24</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-headroom\"></span>\n<h3>headroom</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> The gap between how a segment performs at a journey step today and a realistic level it could reach, which bounds how much an improvement there can add.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> A checkout step where 40% of users succeed and comparable products reach 70% has a lot of headroom.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> A step where 95% of people already succeed, however annoying it is to the other 5%.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C08</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-hippo\"></span>\n<h3>HiPPO</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> The highest-paid person's opinion, deciding by seniority instead of an evidence rule agreed in advance.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> A roadmap set in a meeting that reverses when a senior person changes their mind.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> A decision made by a rule agreed before a test, with a senior person committing to the result.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C29</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-hiring-committee\"></span>\n<h3>hiring committee</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> A group, separate from the interviewers, that reviews written interview feedback and decides whether to hire.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> A panel that reads each interviewer's written evidence about a candidate it never met.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> A hiring manager deciding alone after the interviews.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C33</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-holdout\"></span>\n<h3>holdout</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> A group deliberately kept from a change (or a set of changes) for a period so that its outcome serves as the comparison, often over a long horizon.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> A 2% group of members kept on the old experience for months to see whether a quarter's launches added up.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> A group left out only because it was hard to reach.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C14</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-human-in-the-loop\"></span>\n<h3>human in the loop</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> A design in which a person reviews or approves an AI output or action before it takes effect.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> The user confirms an order summary before an assistant places it.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> An agent that sends emails with no review.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C24</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-hypothesis\"></span>\n<h3>hypothesis</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> A specific, testable statement about a cause or an effect that could turn out to be false.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> &quot;The drop comes from Android users on the latest release.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> &quot;Something is wrong with Android.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C03</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-idempotency\"></span>\n<h3>idempotency</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> The property that doing an operation more than once has the same effect as doing it once; an idempotency key lets a server recognize a retried request and return the first result instead of repeating the action.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> A retried 'charge $50' request returns the original confirmation and charges the card once.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> A retry that charges the customer $100.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C21</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-influence-without-authority\"></span>\n<h3>influence without authority</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> Getting people to act on a proposal when you are not their manager and cannot require it, by understanding their incentives, finding a shared goal, bringing evidence, pre-wiring and making the ask easy.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> Securing engineering support by showing the shared goal, trading scope and asking for a half-day estimate.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> Pressure, false urgency or hiding the costs.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C32</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-information-architecture\"></span>\n<h3>information architecture</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> The way content and functions are organized, grouped and labeled so people can find them.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> Deciding that notification controls sit under a clearly named Settings group with a search box.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> The visual style of the screen.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C10</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-input-metric\"></span>\n<h3>input metric</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> A metric a team can move directly that drives the north star.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> Share of new teams that invite a colleague in week one.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> The north star itself.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C05</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-instrumentation\"></span>\n<h3>instrumentation</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> The code that records user actions and system events as data, which is how a metric becomes a number.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> An invite_sent event with its time, user and channel.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> A dashboard that displays numbers computed from events.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C05</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-inter-rater-agreement\"></span>\n<h3>inter-rater agreement</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> The extent to which independent reviewers give the same judgment, corrected for the agreement expected by chance.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> 94% raw agreement that is a kappa of 0.63 once chance is removed.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> Raw percent agreement on a set where nearly every item is good.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C25</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-interference\"></span>\n<h3>interference</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> A situation in which one unit's treatment affects another unit's outcome, breaking independence between groups.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> In a marketplace, discounts for buyers in the test group change prices for buyers in the control group.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> A feature that only affects the user who sees it.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C14</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-internal-platform\"></span>\n<h3>internal platform</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> A product, such as a service, tool or set of interfaces, whose customers are other teams within the same company, who cannot easily be forced to be delighted and adopt it when it saves them time or risk.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> A shared experimentation service used by forty product teams.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> A consumer product sold to the public.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C27</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-interview-loop\"></span>\n<h3>interview loop</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> The set of interviews a candidate completes in one hiring process, usually in a single day or a few days, whose feedback is combined into one decision.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> Four 45-minute interviews on product design, metrics, a technical conversation and behavioral questions, followed by a debrief.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> A single phone call with a recruiter.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C33</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-inversion\"></span>\n<h3>inversion</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> Solving a problem by asking what would guarantee failure and then avoiding it.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> Listing how to make users mute every notification, then designing the opposite.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> Brainstorming features that might delight users.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C02</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-issue-tree\"></span>\n<h3>issue tree</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> A hierarchy that breaks a question into MECE branches, down to items that can be tested.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> Revenue drop split into traffic, conversion and revenue per order, then each split further.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> A flat list of ideas with no hierarchy.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C03</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-jobs-to-be-done\"></span>\n<h3>jobs to be done</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> A lens that describes the progress a user is trying to make in a situation, which the product is hired to help with.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> &quot;Help me arrive at a meeting on time without stress.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> &quot;Users aged 25 to 34.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C06</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-kill-criteria\"></span>\n<h3>kill criteria</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> Conditions, set in advance of a test or project, under which the team agrees to stop; written first to prevent moving the goalposts.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> Stop if week-8 retention is below 8% or fewer than 30% of surveyed users would be very disappointed without the product.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> A decision to stop made after seeing results and chosen to justify an existing preference.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C26</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-know-your-customer\"></span>\n<h3>know your customer</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> The process of verifying the identity of a customer, and for businesses their ownership and activity, required by financial regulation along with related monitoring obligations.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> An identity and address check before a payments account is enabled.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> A marketing survey about customer preferences.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C27</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-label\"></span>\n<h3>label</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> The outcome a model is trying to predict for each training example.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> Marked as spam; watched to the end; later disputed as fraud.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> An input attribute such as the time of day.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C23</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-lagging-indicator\"></span>\n<h3>lagging indicator</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> A measure that reflects results after they have happened.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> Quarterly churn.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> Number of onboarding steps completed on day one.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C05</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-latency\"></span>\n<h3>latency</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> The time between a request and its response.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> The delay between tapping search and seeing results.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> How many requests a system can handle per second.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C21</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-leading-indicator\"></span>\n<h3>leading indicator</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> A measure that moves before the outcome it predicts.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> First-week activity, as a predictor of 90-day retention.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> Last quarter's revenue.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C05</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-legacy-user\"></span>\n<h3>legacy user</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> A user or customer who depends on the current behavior of a product (habits, integrations, stored data or contracts), so that a change creates cost for them even when it benefits others.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> An enterprise customer whose weekly reporting scripts read the current export columns.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> A brand-new user with no habits or integrations to break.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C08</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-lifetime-value\"></span>\n<h3>lifetime value</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> The total contribution a customer is expected to generate over their life with the product; in a simple model, contribution per month divided by monthly churn. Abbreviated LTV.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> $6 a month of contribution at 5% monthly churn gives a lifetime value of $120.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> Total revenue to date from a customer.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C20</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-liquidity\"></span>\n<h3>liquidity</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> In a marketplace, the likelihood that a participant on one side finds what they want from the other side quickly; measured by the share of searches with an available relevant result, conversion given a result and supplier utilization.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> 40% of searches in a city show an available listing and hosts book 0.25 times a week.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> The number of sellers who have signed up.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C27</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-llm\"></span>\n<h3>large language model</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> A model trained on large amounts of text to predict the next token, which can generate and transform language.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> A model that drafts an email reply from a prompt.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> A database that returns stored answers.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C24</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-local-optimum\"></span>\n<h3>local optimum</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> The best result reachable by small changes from the current position, which may be far worse than a result reachable by a different approach.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> A signup flow tuned by copy tests to a plateau while a different first screen would double conversion.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> The best possible result across all approaches.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C02</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-localization\"></span>\n<h3>localization</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> Adapting a product to a market's language, norms, infrastructure and rules so that it works and feels right there; translation is only its first layer.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> Showing a fare upfront, accepting cash and letting riders describe a pickup by landmark when street numbers are not used.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> Translating the interface text and leaving the sign-up, payment and address flows unchanged.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C09</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-mece\"></span>\n<h3>MECE</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> A way of splitting a problem into parts that do not overlap and together cover the whole.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> Splitting signups by platform: iOS, Android, web.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> Splitting signups into &quot;mobile&quot;, &quot;iOS&quot; and &quot;new users&quot;, which overlap and leave gaps.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C03</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-message-queue\"></span>\n<h3>message queue</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> A holding area for tasks or messages that workers process later, so the part of the system that receives a request need not wait for slow work.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> A confirmation email waiting in a queue while the user already sees a quick response.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> A function that blocks until the email is sent.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C21</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-metric\"></span>\n<h3>metric</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> A defined quantity, measured over a stated population and time window, that tracks one aspect of product performance.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> &quot;Share of new users who complete a first purchase within seven days.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> &quot;Engagement.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C05</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-metric-diagnosis\"></span>\n<h3>metric diagnosis</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> A structured investigation into why a metric changed, ending in a cause, a decision and a communication.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> Confirming a signup drop is real, isolating it to one platform and one release, and recommending a rollback.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> Guessing a cause and proposing a fix.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C13</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-metric-tree\"></span>\n<h3>metric tree</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> A diagram that decomposes a metric into components that multiply or add up to it.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> Orders = visitors × conversion rate; each factor then decomposed further.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> A dashboard of unrelated metrics.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C05</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-minimum-detectable-effect\"></span>\n<h3>minimum detectable effect</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> The smallest change an experiment is designed to detect reliably.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> Sizing a test to detect a one-point lift in conversion.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> The change the team hopes for.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C14</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-mix-shift\"></span>\n<h3>mix shift</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> A change in an overall metric caused by a change in the composition of users or traffic rather than a change in behavior within groups.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> Average order value falls because more new, low-spend users joined.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> Average order value falling because prices were cut.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C13</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-moat\"></span>\n<h3>moat</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> A durable advantage that protects a business from competitors.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> Accumulated data that makes recommendations better with each user.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> Being first to launch.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C18</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-mock-interview\"></span>\n<h3>mock interview</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> A practice interview run under realistic conditions (the real format, time limit and tools, with no pausing or hints) and scored afterward against a rubric.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> A timed 45-minute product round with a partner who probes three levels deep and scores independently.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> Rereading a chapter or watching a recorded sample interview.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C34</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-model\"></span>\n<h3>model</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> A function fitted to data to predict something about new cases, such as whether a message is spam or which items a person will value.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> A fraud model that gives each payment a risk score.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> A hand-written rule such as hold any payment above $10,000 from a new account.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C23</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-model-card\"></span>\n<h3>model card</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> A short document stating a model's intended use, data, evaluation results, limitations and risks.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> A page describing a summarizer's languages, tested error rates and known failure cases.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> A marketing description of a model's capabilities.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C25</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-model-graded-eval\"></span>\n<h3>model-graded eval</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> An evaluation in which a language model scores another model's outputs against a rubric; fast and cheap, and it must be calibrated against human judgments because it can miss real failures.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> A grader model that flags 80 of 1,000 outputs and catches 70 of the 100 that humans found bad.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> Human reviewers scoring outputs against the same rubric.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C25</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-multi-homing\"></span>\n<h3>multi-homing</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> Participants using several platforms at the same time, which weakens lock-in and network-effect advantages.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> A driver running two ride-hailing apps at once.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> Users who can only reach their friends on one app.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C19</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-multiple-comparisons\"></span>\n<h3>multiple comparisons</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> The inflation of false-positive chances when many tests or segments are examined and the most striking result is reported.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> Slicing a result 20 ways at a 5% threshold gives about a 64% chance of at least one significant segment when nothing is going on.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> A single comparison planned in advance.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C12</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-mvp\"></span>\n<h3>minimum viable product</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> The smallest thing that can test the riskiest assumption with real users.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> A manual concierge service run by hand to test demand before building software.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> A full product with a few features removed for time.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C15</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-net-revenue-retention\"></span>\n<h3>net revenue retention</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> The revenue from a cohort of customers a year later, including expansion and net of churn and downgrades, as a percentage of their revenue at the start; above 100 percent means existing customers grow without new customers.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> A cohort paying $1,000,000 that pays $1,100,000 a year later has NRR of 110%.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> The share of customers who did not cancel.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C27</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-network-effect\"></span>\n<h3>network effect</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> A property in which each additional user increases the product's value to the other users.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> A messaging app becomes more useful as more of a person's contacts join.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> A product that is cheaper at higher volume.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C18</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-non-goal\"></span>\n<h3>non-goal</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> Something explicitly declared out of scope for a project.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> &quot;This release will not support offline mode.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> A requirement postponed without saying so.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C16</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-north-star-metric\"></span>\n<h3>north star metric</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> The single metric that best captures the value users get from a product and that predicts long-term success.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> Weekly active teams that send messages, for a team chat product.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> Page views.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C05</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-novelty-effect\"></span>\n<h3>novelty effect</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> A temporary change in behavior because something is new, which fades as the novelty wears off; the mirror image, a temporary dip from change aversion, is handled the same way.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> A redesigned row lifts viewing by 6% in week one and settles near 1% by week five.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> A stable lift that is the same in week one and week six.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C14</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-nudge\"></span>\n<h3>nudge</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> A design choice that steers behavior toward an option without forbidding others or changing incentives sharply.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> An unchecked, clearly worded opt-in asked at a moment when the value is clear.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> A pre-checked box that signs people up for charges and hides the way out.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C10</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-offline-evaluation\"></span>\n<h3>offline evaluation</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> Judging a model on historical data without showing it to users; fast and cheap and unable to show how users react to new results.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> Measuring the error of predicted ratings on held-out past ratings.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> An online experiment with real users.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C23</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-okr\"></span>\n<h3>OKR</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> A goal-setting method pairing a qualitative objective with a small number of measurable key results that are outcomes, not tasks.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> Objective: new users reach first success quickly. Key result: raise the share completing the first task within 10 minutes from 38% to 55%.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> Key results that read 'launch the tutorial' and 'complete the redesign.'</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C18</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-on-device-processing\"></span>\n<h3>on-device processing</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> Running computation on the user's own device instead of sending data to a server.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> Recognizing a voice command on the phone without sending the audio away.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> Uploading audio to a server for transcription.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C21</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-opportunity-cost\"></span>\n<h3>opportunity cost</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> The value of the best alternative given up when a choice is made.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> Building the custom feature displaces a planned item expected to return $1,200,000 a year.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> The cost of the chosen option measured on its own.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C15</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-order-of-magnitude\"></span>\n<h3>order of magnitude</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> A factor of ten, used to judge whether a quantity is about a thousand, a million or a billion.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> Two estimates that differ by a factor of one hundred signal a unit error or a missing factor.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> The third significant digit of an answer.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C04</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-overfitting\"></span>\n<h3>overfitting</h3>\n";
